WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.790
KAZ SATO: Can I start?

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:09.910
OK, thank you.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.540
Thank you so much for taking
your time for my session.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.800
What is ML Ops?

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.890
The best practices
for DevOps for ML.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:17.910
I'm Kaz.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.260
I'm a developer advocate.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.095
So it's like an evangelist
for developers and engineers.

00:00:22.095 --> 00:00:24.650
I'm focusing on data
analytics products,

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.660
and like TensorFlow machine
learning engine and BigQuery.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.500
I have been working at
Google for about seven years.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.120
And what is ML Ops?

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.910
Actually, I and my colleague
have created this word.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.660
There is no ML Ops
word ever in Ops.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.590
So that stands for
the DevOps for ML.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:48.160
And I have been collecting
this concept or topics

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.780
of the operating production
machine learning systems

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.300
for a couple of years because
I have been keep hearing

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.060
from the ML experts
that launching is easy,

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.610
but the operating is hard--

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.740
from customers and
internal ML experts.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.060
There's one ML
expert in Google who

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.480
has been supporting one
of the largest ML service

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.250
inside Google for many years.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.420
And he said launching the first
version of ML system is easy,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.440
but operating it for many
years is the hardest part.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.040
So I thought maybe the
practices for DevOps

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:30.110
could work well for building
production quality ML systems.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:32.470
I copied this definition
of DevOps from Wikipedia.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:37.180
It says it's all about unifying
your Dev part and Ops part,

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.000
and strongly advocate
automation and monitoring

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.610
for all steps for the systems.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:46.900
So you can replace that part,
IT part or system part with ML.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.520
So to build the production
quality ML systems,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.820
it's important to unify the
ML development and operations,

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:59.740
and advocate automation and
monitoring of ML systems.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:05.380
And we have been publishing a
couple of papers in the past,

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.590
such as the ML, "Machine
Learning, the High-Interest

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.199
Credit Card of Technical Debt."

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:12.940
I call it as an "ML
Technical Debt" paper.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.540
That was written by an
ML expert inside Google

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.430
who has been operating
the large scale

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.880
machine learning production
system for many years.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.740
And they have
learned many things

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.400
in the past over 10, 15
years, and they have written

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.130
the best practice as a paper.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:33.820
Another important paper is the
"Rules of Machine Learning,"

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.360
or "Rules of ML" paper.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.730
And with those papers added
with the other many episodes

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:44.770
I have collected with the
customers and customer

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:48.640
engineers, I'm collecting many
anti-patterns and solutions

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.930
for the production
machine learning systems.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.550
And I have three
agendas for today.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.540
One is the anti-patterns
for development time,

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.470
patterns for the deployment,
and operation anti-patterns.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.140
So let's start with the
development anti-patterns.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.400
The first pattern
is the ML superhero.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.180
Maybe you have seen
him in your company.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.030
He's a superhero because he's
a researcher who understands

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.070
everything, like a
mathematical model, what's

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.140
happening inside his model.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:25.640
But he also has a skill set
to built his own batch Spark

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.040
cluster by himself,
to do everything

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.530
from preparation,
data prep preparation,

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.860
or the transformation training.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.440
And also, he has a
skill set, and build

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:39.530
and operating the infrastructure
by using Kubernetes,

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:42.080
writing the Docker
file by himself.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.530
And also, he can be
a product manager

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:48.140
who can write the
design dock by himself.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.750
And also, he is a very
fluent in communicating

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:55.270
with the stakeholders,
like CXOs and executives.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:58.950
And he does it from the
proof of concept phase

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.630
to the production phase.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:02.340
He's a Superman.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:03.710
But he doesn't scale.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:10.370
So your first project would
be awesome for depending

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:11.630
on that kind of hero.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.690
But if an enterprise
or startup wants

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:19.589
to reproduce the same
success with ML systems

00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:23.450
with many different features
or products or divisions,

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:28.050
then you have to hire that kind
of superhero, tens of them.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:31.980
So instead, you have to
build a scalable team

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:36.210
by splitting your roles
into much simpler roles.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.450
So you can have multiple roles,
such as the ML researcher

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.300
or data engineer, infrastructure
engineer, product manager,

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.670
and business decision-maker.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.560
Now, let me show one
example of the team.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.390
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:39.034
[END PLAYBACK]

00:05:39.034 --> 00:05:40.700
KAZ SATO: So that was
the demonstration,

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.110
we had built for the
I/O Next last year.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.210
So some of you may have
seen this last year.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.710
And this was a demonstration,
but for us, it's

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:52.390
a production machine
learning system.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:53.500
And I designed it.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:56.890
I had written a rough design
doc for this by myself.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:00.820
And worked with the vendor
called BrainPad in Japan

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.610
who has expertise on building
the production machine learning

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:05.840
system for many years.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.370
So because I wanted to
make a production quality

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:14.080
demonstration, so I asked
them to build a team for this

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.840
because the I/O is
not only the one

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.640
venue for these demonstrations.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.800
We had to present
this demonstration

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.150
at many large events, like
Next San Francisco, Next Tokyo,

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:28.400
and India, and
many other places.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.700
So we cannot rely
on the ML superhero.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:35.470
So BrainPad had created a
team with a researcher who

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:37.900
will build the Word2Vec
model running on TensorFlow

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:41.960
for the candy recommendation,
and also the image recognition

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:43.390
and [INAUDIBLE] while running.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:49.005
And also a software engineer
who uses the ML APIs, speech

00:06:49.005 --> 00:06:51.720
to text API and inner API.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.010
Hardware engineer
who focuses on how

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.380
to control the robot precisely.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:58.960
And project manager
who leads the team.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.260
And I was the business
decision-maker,

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:05.290
communicating with all the other
stakeholders, like marketing,

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.936
PR team, and legal, everything.

00:07:07.936 --> 00:07:11.050
So that went well
because BrainPad

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:15.940
has practice to
create very tightly

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.790
coupled teams with the
researchers and engineers

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:20.410
as one team.

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:25.210
But sometimes you have
this problem, a black box

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.460
that nobody understands.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:29.710
If you create a
machine learning team

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:33.820
with the researchers and
engineers and info engineers,

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:37.960
it's possible that nobody
understand the whole system.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:41.450
Because researchers would
create the first prototype

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:45.970
with his or her
Jupyter Notebook.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:48.440
And then engineer
takes that code,

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:52.660
does the refactoring on that
to bring it to production.

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:56.440
And then researchers
would not follow

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:58.510
what's happening in
the production code.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:00.280
At the same time,
maybe engineer don't

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:02.650
match knowledge about
what's happening inside

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:04.210
the model itself.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.180
Then nobody understands
what's happening

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:08.850
inside the whole
system, and it's

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:14.480
really hard for the
optimization as a whole system.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:17.310
So the "ML Technical
Debt" paper says

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:20.200
at Google, a hybrid
research approach where

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.410
engineers and researchers
are embedded together

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:25.780
on the same team,
that has helped

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:28.580
reduce this source of
friction significantly.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:31.180
So not only building
a scalable team,

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:35.360
but also, it's really
important to have tight,

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.549
close communication
between the researcher

00:08:39.549 --> 00:08:42.059
and engineer to
understand everything,

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:47.770
and share all the same
results, like a code and model.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.960
And, for example,
maybe engineer can

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:53.650
help introduce a new scalable
platform, like Cloud ML Engine,

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:56.830
working with the researchers.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:59.890
You can do that because we
have introduced a new feature,

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:03.620
scikit-learn and XGBoost
spot on the Cloud ML Engine,

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.530
not only in TensorFlow.

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:08.110
So, for example,
researchers can start

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:10.840
using the scikit-learn
on Jupyter Notebook

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:12.970
to define their model.

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:15.700
And then, engineer work
together to port it

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.530
to the Cloud ML Engine.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:20.230
So let me show some
video that is created

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:24.700
by Steve Greenberg,
my colleague, how

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:28.490
you can use the scikit-learn
on machine learning engine.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:30.580
So for the first part,
maybe the researchers

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:33.260
can get started with
Jupyter Notebook

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:36.460
to find the usual
scikit-learn model.

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:40.300
So you can use the BigQuery
for getting the training

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:42.100
data from database.

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:44.920
And you can use the
ordinary API on scikit-learn

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:50.120
to do the pre-processing or
splitting data, everything.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:54.070
Yeah, here it is splitting
the data to the training data

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.550
and test data.

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:59.170
And you can also use the
pipeline API of scikit-learn

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.250
to build the pipeline
for training.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:06.500
And then call the fit
function to train the model,

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:09.284
and try the prediction
and get the result.

00:10:09.284 --> 00:10:10.700
So this is the
part of researchers

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.370
can do with the usual practice.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:17.270
And this is where you can get
the help from the ML engineer

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:20.190
or your infra engineer.

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:22.850
This is where you are copying
the scikit-learn model

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:24.380
to the Google Cloud
[INAUDIBLE] so

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.650
that the ML engine can read it.

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:30.530
And also, you can
use ML engine command

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:34.160
to define a new version with
that scikit-learn model running

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:36.790
on ML engine.

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:37.340
Like this.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.490
And finally, you can try
learning it with ML engine.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:49.390
So it is trying to
try the prediction

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:52.140
with the scikit-learn
model running on the cloud.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:55.500
And you can get
here same accuracy.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:59.370
An important point here is
that, by using the ML engine

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:02.220
as the prediction
serving infrastructure

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.220
for your scikit-learn
model, you don't

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:07.790
have to think much about the
scalability and variability

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:09.090
by yourself.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:11.460
Usually there's a huge
gap between the POC

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:15.150
model, POC systems, and
the production systems.

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:17.310
You have to think
about how to build

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:20.074
your cluster with the
Kubernetes and containers,

00:11:20.074 --> 00:11:22.490
and you have to think about
the lifecycle of each instance

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:23.550
and containers.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.370
But by using ML engine,
it's much easier.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:29.790
And engineers can help that,
working with the researchers

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:32.883
to bring the POC
code into production.

00:11:35.841 --> 00:11:38.440
So, next slide.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:43.460
Next pattern is CACE, or Change
Anything, Changes Everything.

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:45.310
It's a very unique
characteristic

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:47.330
or the behavior of ML systems.

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:51.280
It's an entanglement you
can find in ML systems.

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.980
That means a single change,
one change to one feature,

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:56.760
could affect all of
the other features.

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:58.960
Or single changes in
the hyper-parameters

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:03.340
or any way you prepare your
training data or feature,

00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:05.740
that could change the
behavior of everything,

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:11.070
like how you can do your
regularization or running rate

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:12.940
or sampling or thresholds.

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:15.860
That could change the
behavior of the whole system.

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:20.980
That's the reason why the ML
experts said launching is easy,

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:24.430
but operating is hard.

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:28.210
"Rules of ML" paper
defines Rule 14,

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:32.410
starting with an interpretable
model makes debugging easier.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:35.620
And there's another
rule, it's Rule 40.

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:38.810
Keep ensembles simple.

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:39.855
What is a simple model?

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:45.720
The deep learning
model is so attractive,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:47.910
and you can say to
the business people

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:51.000
that we are using deep
learning with an AI system.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:55.240
But sometimes the deep
model makes things complex.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:59.310
So if you want to use deep model
in your production systems,

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:04.084
you can use that judiciously
because linear model is much,

00:13:04.084 --> 00:13:05.760
much easier for debugging.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.280
It's much easier
to train the model

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:12.180
and get the global
optimum, whereas if you're

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:14.910
using the deep model, usually
the model is non-complex.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:18.060
That means you are not sure
if you're getting the best

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:20.730
result from training or not.

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:23.280
You have to make many trials.

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.000
And also, it's important to
make everything interpretable

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:31.900
by humans, not
having a black box.

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:34.200
Another pattern here
or solution here

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:37.350
is to use ensembled models,
rather than using a huge,

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.030
one monolithic model.

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:43.260
So you could have
one big, deep model

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:46.740
that takes hundreds of the
features from the system.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:50.750
Then it's really hard to debug
or troubleshoot against that.

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:55.290
So one practice you can use is
to have a smaller model, small

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.090
and simpler model.

00:13:57.090 --> 00:14:00.930
Works just like a parts
factory or automobile factory.

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:03.240
And you could have
another model that

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:08.010
assembles all the synthesized
features from those factories.

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:10.920
So it works as an
assembly plant.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:14.730
And it's also important to
have everything semantically

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:20.240
interpretable model so that the
persons for troubleshooting,

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:22.800
like operators, can
easily find what

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.490
will be the problem, what will
be the cause of the problem.

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:27.840
Is it this model, this
model, or this model?

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:29.760
Rather than having
a gigantic, one

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:33.570
monolithic model where the
trouble would be embedded

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:36.630
somewhere in the model.

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:39.570
I wanted to show
one demonstration.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:40.160
It's Konpeki.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:44.430
It's an image classifier for
the use case built by Aucnet.

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:47.370
Aucnet is the largest
provider for the real time

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.050
auction for use case in Japan.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:53.700
And they handle over 5
million auctions per year.

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:57.990
And they have built a TensorFlow
based image classifier

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:00.480
specialized for the use case.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:06.270
So the users can upload all
the different pictures of a car

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:11.260
from different angles, like from
front and back or side or tires

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:12.840
or steering wheels.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:18.660
And then systems classify
that, and you can get your car

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:24.360
make and model name and
classification of these images

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:25.159
at high accuracy.

00:15:25.159 --> 00:15:25.950
They have built it.

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:29.010
And it has been on production
for over two years.

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:35.640
And one thing they did when they
were building the production

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:38.280
system was to split the model.

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:44.010
So Aucnet initially created one
big deep learning model that

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:46.500
takes all the features into it.

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:51.220
But it ended up always splitting
the model into smaller models.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:54.010
So they found they
were able to do

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:57.330
better troubleshooting
or better optimization

00:15:57.330 --> 00:15:59.721
with the whole system.

00:15:59.721 --> 00:16:02.020
Ah, sorry about that.

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:03.677
Why is that?

00:16:03.677 --> 00:16:04.552
SPEAKER 1: It's back.

00:16:04.552 --> 00:16:06.544
KAZ SATO: Mmm.

00:16:06.544 --> 00:16:07.540
Does it work?

00:16:13.016 --> 00:16:13.516
Ah, sorry.

00:16:13.516 --> 00:16:15.520
OK.

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:17.410
Let me try it.

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:19.510
So it's much easier for
the production systems

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:21.730
in troubleshooting.

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:27.310
So that was the pattern you can
see in your development phase.

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:31.100
And let's take a look at the
deployment anti-patterns.

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:35.120
The first pattern is lack
of ML lifecycle management.

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:37.350
The one scenario could
be, maybe a researcher

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:40.800
would just start writing
the POC with Notebook.

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:43.430
And he or she does
everything on it from POC

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:46.770
to production, like
data preparation,

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.440
transformation, training,
validation, and serving,

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:50.740
and deploying.

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:55.020
And researcher got high accuracy
on the production service.

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:58.470
But he or she would
forget about the system

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:00.360
after one or two years.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:02.910
So after one year or two
years, maybe somebody

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:06.780
found the accuracy
dropping slowly.

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:10.020
And director of the
business asks the researcher

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:15.270
to update the model
ASAP, like by tomorrow.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:19.109
The researcher will somehow
finds the old Notebook

00:17:19.109 --> 00:17:23.040
on laptop, tries to remember
how to go through, quickly,

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:28.200
all these cells, and do
every process manually.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:32.820
And wonders, why am I doing
the emergency plumbing?

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:37.200
He or she is a researcher,
and this is not his job.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:40.810
So the solution is to think
about ML life cycle management.

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:43.810
So integrated job
management, job

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:49.070
orchestration so that you
can define all the components

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:52.360
as a part of the whole
job of orchestration,

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:55.300
starting from the data
ingestion, analysis,

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:58.300
and validation, training,
and serving, and logging,

00:17:58.300 --> 00:17:59.980
and monitoring.

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:03.920
Not as the ad hoc script
running on the Jupyter Notebook.

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:09.620
You have to build everything
as a continuous deployment

00:18:09.620 --> 00:18:13.820
on repeatable infrastructure,
not depending on the researcher

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:15.670
only.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:19.760
One example of building that
kind of workflow is using Argo.

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:24.550
Argo is an open-source tool for
workflow as a workflow engine

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:25.950
for Kubernetes containers.

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:28.720
So you can use this
tool to build this kind

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:32.190
of orchestration for ML system.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:35.490
And actually, we have tried
that for the one demonstration

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:37.560
called AI in Motion.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:39.900
This is a demonstration you
can find in the showcase

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:45.900
area in the Moscone South where
we have the robot balls rolling

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:47.430
on the table.

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:52.200
And we have a smartphone,
Pixel 2, up above

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:55.500
that takes all the
images of the robot

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:59.340
balls, and running
the object detection

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:02.650
model of the
TensorFlow, and control

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:05.370
the robots by using SDK.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:09.120
And this object detection
model was trained at the cloud

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:10.620
by using ML engine.

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:14.670
And it also uses
Reinforcement Learning, RL,

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:16.200
to control the balls.

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:20.490
So if you're interested,
you can play by yourself

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:23.340
by going to the showcase.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:26.430
And we had to build the
ML pipeline or ML workflow

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:30.930
because, as you can
see, it's very important

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:34.770
to have high accuracy
on object detection

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:36.780
at every different venue.

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:40.040
So each venue would have a
different lighting environment,

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:43.830
like white lighting or
color lighting, or strengths

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:46.150
of the lighting
would be different.

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:50.310
So it's important to be
able to retrain the model

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:53.550
and to get the best
result at each venue.

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:56.230
So as long as you're
using the Jupyter Notebook

00:19:56.230 --> 00:19:58.200
and run using the
manual process,

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:03.520
it's not scalable and
portable as a demonstration.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:06.630
So for this demonstration,
we have built the ML pipeline

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:08.360
by using Argo.

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:12.000
So starting from loading the
training data and training

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:14.950
and evaluation, and
also, in this case,

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.310
we have built a pipeline
for converting everything

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:19.660
into a TensorFlow like model.

00:20:19.660 --> 00:20:21.810
Converting the TensorFlow
like model and deploy

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:24.990
the TensorFlow, write files
for the mobile devices.

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:28.490
And let me show some examples.

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:33.170
This is the working
Argo user interface

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:35.660
for the AI in Motion
demonstrations.

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:38.270
And the UI works
just like the UI

00:20:38.270 --> 00:20:39.840
you have in your
cloud data flow.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:42.620
So if you click one
of the workflows,

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:46.320
you can see what's the current
status of the workflow.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:49.310
Like what's the status?

00:20:49.310 --> 00:20:51.620
Has it succeeded
or has it failed?

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:54.350
Or starting time, end
time, and duration.

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:57.080
Just like the things you can
see on the cloud data flow.

00:20:57.080 --> 00:21:00.590
So by having this kind
of orchestration tool,

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:03.980
then you don't have to go
through all the Jupyter

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:05.960
Notebook processes
by yourself manually.

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:09.310
Instead, you can have someone,
like operators or engineers

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:13.510
or the researchers, pressing
the buttons of the workflow,

00:21:13.510 --> 00:21:18.230
the Argo, to go through all
the repeatable processes

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:19.640
between the model,
and deploy it.

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:28.810
Another pattern you can
see in the deployment time

00:21:28.810 --> 00:21:31.860
is a lack of data validation.

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:34.660
In the usual,
ordinary IT system,

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:37.150
the behavior of the system
is defined by the program

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:38.620
code you have written.

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:42.570
So you have to write a
unit test to validate

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:45.230
the behavior of your system.

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:47.170
But what about the
machine learning system?

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:49.480
Because you are not writing
the code for defining

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:51.580
the behavior of your system.

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:56.410
Instead, your system would
be using training data

00:21:56.410 --> 00:21:59.650
to define the behavior
of the system.

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:03.470
And how do you validate that?

00:22:03.470 --> 00:22:07.030
The solution could be the
data validation tools.

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:12.460
And one option is the TFX,
or TensorFlow Extended.

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:14.140
That is an open-source
tool provided

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:16.150
by the TensorFlow
team that provides

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:21.370
multiple different useful
tools for the ML Ops practices,

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:23.650
such as the transformation
for the data,

00:22:23.650 --> 00:22:27.426
or the model analysis that
I'll be talking about later,

00:22:27.426 --> 00:22:29.230
or the serving.

00:22:29.230 --> 00:22:32.050
And one of those features
is data validation.

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:34.000
At this point, we
haven't open-sourced

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:36.700
the data validation
part of yet, but we

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:41.920
hope to have it soon
in the near future.

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:44.500
By using TFX for
data validation,

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:47.320
you can easily define
the configuration file

00:22:47.320 --> 00:22:51.250
for the data validation,
such as the type and domain

00:22:51.250 --> 00:22:53.860
for each feature or
input data, whether it's

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:56.970
a string or integer or
what kind of range it has.

00:22:56.970 --> 00:23:01.900
It presents instances of
each of values or variancy

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:07.390
or distribution by comparing the
divergence between two values.

00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:14.150
And if it fits into a certain
threshold, then it's OK.

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:16.580
Otherwise, you have to
check whether your training

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:19.630
data is OK or not.

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:23.440
And "TFX" paper
says we want to user

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:25.900
to treat data errors
with the same rigor

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:28.750
and care that deal
with debugs in code.

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:33.030
Because the data is your
code in your ML system.

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:36.040
Actually, the Google Play team
has introduced the practice

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:38.020
of data validation
so that they were

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:42.370
able to increase the install
rate for applications for 2%.

00:23:45.610 --> 00:23:48.620
So lastly, I'd like to
discuss about anti-patterns

00:23:48.620 --> 00:23:52.000
you can find in operation time.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:53.410
Ah, sorry about that.

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:54.510
SPEAKER 2: It's OK.

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:56.321
KAZ SATO: Why is that?

00:23:56.321 --> 00:23:57.080
Is it working?

00:24:02.508 --> 00:24:03.008
OK.

00:24:10.418 --> 00:24:11.406
Now updating.

00:24:39.150 --> 00:24:40.860
Maybe, yeah, we
try connecting to--

00:24:44.842 --> 00:24:46.217
SPEAKER 2: It's
working properly.

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:50.460
KAZ SATO: Is it working?

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:51.660
SPEAKER 1: I think it is.

00:24:51.660 --> 00:24:53.618
KAZ SATO: But it looks
like it's not my laptop.

00:24:59.660 --> 00:25:01.780
Looks like they switched.

00:25:01.780 --> 00:25:04.470
Yeah, I can continue
with the other laptop.

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:08.180
And can I use your clicker?

00:25:08.180 --> 00:25:11.120
Where's clicker?

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:14.185
Oh yeah, I have it.

00:25:14.185 --> 00:25:15.010
Not working.

00:25:21.758 --> 00:25:23.990
It's slow.

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:25.340
It's slow, but working.

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:26.270
Sorry about that.

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:28.130
So lack of continuous
monitoring.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:31.530
So any kind of model
would see the drop

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:33.050
of the accuracy over time.

00:25:33.050 --> 00:25:36.990
Maybe it would take
years, or maybe

00:25:36.990 --> 00:25:41.990
quarters or months or hours,
but the accuracy would drop.

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:45.680
And if there's no
practice for your team

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:47.630
for continuous monitoring
on your accuracy

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:51.260
or behavior of your
model, the first person

00:25:51.260 --> 00:25:53.430
who'd notice the
drop of the accuracy

00:25:53.430 --> 00:25:55.850
would be the end
user or consumers.

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:59.120
They would be frustrated with
the performance or experience,

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:01.160
and business team
would notice that.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:05.870
And director asked you to
update the model ASAP again.

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:09.920
So that's what we want to avoid.

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:13.730
This is another pattern,
training-serving skew.

00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:15.950
That means any
single differences,

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:20.780
like differences of the training
data or pre-processing method

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:24.560
or the windows you specify,
between training and serving

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:28.110
or POC to production,
that changes the accuracy.

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:35.690
Another pattern is not knowing
the freshness requirements.

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:39.930
So each ML system would have
its own freshness requirement.

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:42.500
For example, if you want to
build the news aggregation

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:46.370
website, then you may want
to update your ML model

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:50.390
every five minutes to
get the latest updates.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:53.900
E-commerce site
item recommendation

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:56.720
can wait for a couple
of days or a week

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:59.205
until updating the models.

00:26:59.205 --> 00:27:01.580
The natural language processing
for customer satisfaction

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:05.360
measurement may use the same
model for a couple of months,

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:07.240
or may be longer than that.

00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:10.580
Voice recognition doesn't
require any updates

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:11.870
on the model.

00:27:11.870 --> 00:27:14.450
Object detection
sometimes requires

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:18.460
retraining the model for every
setup, as I mentioned in the AI

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:20.990
in Motion case.

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:24.050
And "Rules of ML" paper
says, Rule Number 9,

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:27.720
detect problems before
exporting models.

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.500
Then Rule Number 8, know
the freshness requirements

00:27:30.500 --> 00:27:33.420
for your system.

00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:37.610
And the solution could be
a TFMA, or TensorFlow Model

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:39.510
Analysis.

00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:42.900
That is a new feature
included in TensorFlow

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:47.310
to do analytics on your model.

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:50.510
And by using TFMA,
you can follow

00:27:50.510 --> 00:27:53.940
some practices or
rules, such as Rule 24,

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:56.610
measure the delta
between your models.

00:27:56.610 --> 00:28:01.090
Rather than watching the single
accuracy of the current model,

00:28:01.090 --> 00:28:04.680
you have to see the
changes or trend

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:07.200
between the different the
models in a time series.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:10.620
How the accuracy
will be trending?

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:15.100
Is it increasing or
is it decreasing?

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:18.880
And another practice
you can use with TFMA

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:23.290
is using the sliced metrics
for better model analysis.

00:28:23.290 --> 00:28:27.460
Not only using a single
ROC or AUC number,

00:28:27.460 --> 00:28:30.410
you can use different
types of features,

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:36.040
or different windows
of the features,

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:40.620
to get deeper insight of
the behavior of your model.

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:43.080
Let me show some
example of using TFMA.

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:53.320
This is the step where you're
importing the TFMA library

00:28:53.320 --> 00:28:57.600
and define the slices
by using the API of TFMA

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:00.580
by using the Jupyter Notebook.

00:29:00.580 --> 00:29:04.150
And by defining the
slices, in this case,

00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:08.350
the code is using
Chicago taxi trip data

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:11.650
for training a model
for predicting the taxi

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:14.620
fares in each trip.

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:19.460
And the easiest way is
to get your single ROC

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:22.270
car or the accuracy
on the whole model.

00:29:22.270 --> 00:29:24.850
But by defining on
different slices,

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:33.790
like sliced by the hour in the
day, or day in a week or year,

00:29:33.790 --> 00:29:35.920
you can understand
much more details

00:29:35.920 --> 00:29:39.760
about the behavior
of your model.

00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:42.430
Now it defines the
slices so that you

00:29:42.430 --> 00:29:47.120
can get multiple
accuracies for each slice.

00:29:47.120 --> 00:29:49.710
In this case, it's
a time series.

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:53.460
So you can see different
activities provided

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:56.580
for each slice, hour in a day.

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:05.180
And this highest accuracy
is achieved at noon time

00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:11.780
where you would have
many, many taxi uses.

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:18.330
And once you have confirmed that
your model works as intended

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:21.630
and there's no problem on
the accuracy or behavior,

00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:24.900
then you can use the
versioning of the models

00:30:24.900 --> 00:30:26.800
on machine learning engine.

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:31.560
The machine learning engine
provides some APIs and CLI

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:35.340
to register multiple
versions of your model,

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:37.870
and choose which
one of the models

00:30:37.870 --> 00:30:40.470
you want to use as
a default. So you

00:30:40.470 --> 00:30:44.320
can use those CLIs,
command lines, or the APIs

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:47.640
to define models and use
it as a default model.

00:30:51.730 --> 00:30:53.040
Another rule.

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:56.880
The "Rules of ML" paper says
the second rule is to first

00:30:56.880 --> 00:31:00.430
design and implement metrics.

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:04.420
So if you want to
capture any problems

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:07.400
or want to capture any good
things or good feedback

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:10.320
from customers, define
metrics to capture

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:13.920
all of those behavior
from your ML model.

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:18.870
For example, Google+ team has
been collecting many metrics

00:31:18.870 --> 00:31:21.480
from the behavior
user activities,

00:31:21.480 --> 00:31:23.840
such as how many
expands per read,

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:28.110
or how many reshares per read,
or how many +1s or comments per

00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:29.080
read.

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:33.420
So all of those,
these small metrics,

00:31:33.420 --> 00:31:37.140
can be a good feature for
defining the goodness of post.

00:31:40.070 --> 00:31:43.450
So that was the final
practice and patterns.

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:45.020
So, summary.

00:31:45.020 --> 00:31:47.530
Development anti-patterns.

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:49.660
You cannot rely on a
single ML superhero.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:52.655
Rather than that, you have
to build a scalable team.

00:31:52.655 --> 00:31:58.690
And at the same time, you
cannot build a ML black box.

00:31:58.690 --> 00:32:01.620
And you have to be aware
that ML systems can

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:04.880
have this CACE, or Change
Anything, Change Everything

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:06.694
behavior.

00:32:06.694 --> 00:32:08.235
For deployment, it's
really important

00:32:08.235 --> 00:32:10.900
to think about the machine
learning lifecycle management

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:16.840
by using the tools such as
Argo or the Cloud Composer.

00:32:16.840 --> 00:32:20.490
And also, you have to understand
the behavior of the ML system

00:32:20.490 --> 00:32:24.870
is defined by training
data and your model.

00:32:24.870 --> 00:32:29.370
Lastly, it's very important
to keep monitoring and logging

00:32:29.370 --> 00:32:31.110
your systems.

00:32:31.110 --> 00:32:33.870
So it's very important to set
up your monitoring tool set

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:36.630
and define the
refreshment requirement

00:32:36.630 --> 00:32:38.820
for your machine
learning use cases

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:40.650
by using the tools like TFMA.

00:32:43.366 --> 00:32:45.240
And if you are interested
in those practices,

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:47.370
please take picture
of these links' names

00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:52.960
so you can learn more
about those practices

00:32:52.960 --> 00:32:54.020
with those papers.

00:32:54.020 --> 00:32:57.320
The "ML Technical Debt"
papers, "Rules of ML"

00:32:57.320 --> 00:33:01.690
papers, "TFX,"
and "TFMA" papers.

00:33:01.690 --> 00:33:03.720
And I'd like to
give special thanks

00:33:03.720 --> 00:33:08.310
to Amy Unruh, who's the
colleague of my DA team

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:10.440
who's built this TFMA demo.

00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:13.380
Norihiro Shimoda collected
all the customer episodes.

00:33:13.380 --> 00:33:17.260
And Keisuke built the Argo ML
pipeline for the demonstration.

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:19.910
So with that, thank you so much.

